The full name is Barapasaurus tagorei. The digging went on in the years leading up to 1961, the 100th birth anniversary of [[@tagore|Rabindranath Tagore]]. The team decided to honour the poet, who was also from Bengal.

A mounted skeleton of Barapasaurus stands in the [[@dino-museums|geology museum]] of the Indian Statistical Institute in Kolkata. Because the skull was never found, the head on display is a careful reconstruction based on other early sauropods.

{{story:tagore}}

As ISRO's rockets grew bigger, it needed a larger site. In 1971 it opened a launch range on Sriharikota, a thin island between Pulicat Lake and the sea. It was later named after Satish Dhawan, a former ISRO chairman.

Every Indian rocket that has reached [[orbit|orbit]] has launched from here, including [[@chandrayaan|Chandrayaan-3]] in 2023 and Aditya-L1 later that year. It has two launch pads, and a third is being built for heavier rockets.

{{story:isro-launch}}

The site was chosen for a scientific reason. Thumba lies very close to Earth's magnetic [[equator|equator]], an invisible line where the planet's [[@earth-magnet|magnetic field]] runs flat. Over this line, a river of electric current flows high in the atmosphere, called the equatorial electrojet. Scientists around the world wanted to study it with rockets.

The physicist [[@vikram-sarabhai|Vikram Sarabhai]] picked the spot. The land belonged to a fishing community, with a church dedicated to St Mary Magdalene in the middle. The local bishop agreed to give the church for science, and the villagers agreed to move to a new village with a new church nearby.

{{story:bicycles-history}}

In 1882, Thomas Edison opened the Pearl Street Station in New York, one of the world's first public power stations. At first it supplied a few dozen customers in lower Manhattan, lighting a few hundred lamps. Soon there was a fierce argument over the best kind of current, which you can read about in [[@tesla-edison|Tesla and Edison]].

India wasn't far behind. In 1897, a small [[hydroelectric|hydroelectric]] station at Sidrapong, near Darjeeling, began making power from mountain streams. It was one of the first in Asia.

{{story:tesla-edison}}

Most life jackets are filled with closed-cell foam. Each tiny bubble in the foam is sealed off from its neighbours, so water cannot soak in and the foam keeps its lift even if it is torn. Older jackets used cork or kapok, a fluffy fibre from the seed pods of the kapok tree.

Inflatable life jackets are slim collars that lie flat until needed. Pull a cord and a small cylinder of [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]] fills them. Automatic ones do it themselves when they get wet. They are comfortable for sailors who wear them all day, but they need regular checks, and they are not usually recommended for small children or weak swimmers.

{{story:floating-ships}}

People have known for a very long time that curved glass and water make things look bigger. Around 1011 to 1021, the Arab scientist Ibn al-Haytham, working in Cairo, wrote a huge Book of Optics explaining how light and vision work. It was later translated into Latin and studied across Europe.

By the 1200s, monks in Europe were using polished "reading stones", rounded pieces of crystal laid on top of a page to enlarge the letters. Not long after, craftsmen in Italy began fitting two small lenses into a frame. Those were the first [[@lenses-glasses|spectacles]].

{{story:microscopes}}

Sound travels through air, so any crack lets it through, the way water finds a hole in a bucket. A gap under a door can let in a surprising amount of noise. Electric sockets, air vents and badly fitted windows are common leaks.

Soundproofers seal them with rubber strips and thick, flexible sealant. Studio doors are heavy and close tightly against rubber edges, a bit like a fridge door. Sometimes the cheapest way to make a bedroom quieter is a draught stopper along the bottom of the door.

{{story:noise-cancelling}}

पश्चिमी राजस्थान में मांगणियार और लंगा समुदाय सदियों से पेशेवर संगीतकार रहे हैं। परंपरा से संगीतकारों का हर परिवार कुछ ख़ास [[patron|संरक्षक]] परिवारों के लिए गाता था। वे उनके यहाँ जन्म, शादी और त्योहारों पर गाते, और उनके परिवार का इतिहास गीतों में ज़िंदा रखते थे।

उनका संगीत कमायचा पर बजता है, जो गज से बजने वाला साज़ है और जिसका गोल ढाँचा बकरी की खाल से मढ़ा होता है। ढोलक ताल सँभालती है, और साथ में खड़ताल, यानी लकड़ी के टुकड़ों के जोड़े, जिन्हें एक हाथ में पकड़कर खटखटाया जाता है। कुछ मांगणियार संगीतकार अब दुनिया भर में घूमते हैं और जैसलमेर के पास के रेत के टीलों से बहुत दूर कॉन्सर्ट हॉलों में बजाते हैं।

{{story:storytelling-india}}

The roots go back to [[medieval|medieval]] England. In 1215, the [[@magna-carta|Magna Carta]] promised that free men would not be punished except by the "lawful judgment of their peers", meaning equals, or by the law of the land. Over the following centuries, juries of local people became part of English justice.

When the American colonies broke away, they wrote jury trials into the [[@us-constitution|US Constitution]]. The Sixth Amendment, from 1791, promises a speedy public trial by an impartial jury in criminal cases. The Seventh protects juries in many civil cases.

{{story:magna-carta}}

[[@south-africa|South Africa]] had a system called [[@apartheid|apartheid]], which kept people apart by race. Rugby was loved mostly by white South Africans, and many Black South Africans saw the national team as a symbol of that system.

In 1995, a year after becoming president, [[@nelson-mandela|Nelson Mandela]] walked onto the field at the Rugby World Cup final wearing the team's green jersey. The crowd, mostly white, chanted his name. South Africa won, and many people still call it a turning point.

{{story:nelson-mandela}}

Branching is the most common fractal in living things. A tree trunk splits into branches, then twigs. Your lungs split into airways, then smaller airways, then tiny air sacs, about 20 times over. This packs a huge surface for breathing into your chest.

[[@lightning|Lightning]] forks as it finds the easiest path through the air. Rivers gather streams that gather smaller streams. Seen from an aeroplane, a river system can look like the [[vein|veins]] in a leaf.

{{story:special-effects}}

There is a quick way to estimate how long money takes to double. Divide 72 by the interest rate. At 6% a year, money doubles in about 12 years. At 9%, about 8 years.

The Italian mathematician Luca Pacioli described this rule in a famous book on arithmetic in 1494. He didn't explain where it came from, which suggests merchants were already using it. It works because of the way growth by [[percentage|percentages]] adds up, and it gives a good answer for rates between about 4% and 12%.

{{story:money-banks}}

Cricket bat willow trees are grown for about 15 years or more before they are cut down. The trunk is sawn into short logs and then split into wedge-shaped blocks called clefts. These are dried for months, then shaped, pressed between rollers to harden the face, and fitted with a handle.

English willow is softer and lighter, with a lot of spring. Kashmir willow is harder and heavier, and bats made from it cost much less. That is why many young players in India learn on Kashmir willow. In the Anantnag district of Kashmir, families have made bats for generations.

{{story:lever-lift}}

अगर गेंदबाज़ की गेंद स्टंप्स से टकराकर गिल्लियाँ गिरा दे, तो बल्लेबाज़ आउट (बोल्ड)। अगर कोई फ़ील्डर गेंद को ज़मीन छूने से पहले लपक ले, तब भी आउट (कैच)। और अगर बल्लेबाज़ रन के लिए दौड़ रहा हो, अपनी क्रीज़ में सुरक्षित न पहुँचा हो, और फ़ील्डर विकेट गिरा दे, तो वह रन आउट।

एक और तरीका है एलबीडब्ल्यू, यानी लेग बिफ़ोर विकेट। अगर गेंद स्टंप्स पर लगने वाली थी, पर पहले बल्लेबाज़ के पैड पर लग गई, तो अंपायर उसे आउट दे सकता है। एलबीडब्ल्यू ही वह नियम है जिस पर फ़ैन सबसे ज़्यादा बहस करते हैं। बड़े मैचों में डिसीज़न रिव्यू सिस्टम (डीआरएस) बॉल-ट्रैकिंग [[technology|तकनीक]] से दिखाता है कि गेंद आगे कहाँ जाती। जब दस बल्लेबाज़ आउट हो जाते हैं, तो पारी खत्म हो जाती है, क्योंकि आखिरी बल्लेबाज़ के साथ दौड़ने के लिए कोई साथी नहीं बचता।

{{story:baseball-curve}}

On many streaming services, a play is counted only after about 30 seconds. If a listener skips sooner, it doesn't count.

This rule has changed how songs are written. Long, slow introductions have become rarer, and many pop songs now open with the chorus or a hook, a short catchy bit that grabs attention. Songs have also become shorter on average, because a short song that is replayed twice earns two plays.

{{story:memory}}

In 2016 Neeraj threw 86.48 metres at the World Under-20 Championships in Poland. It was a world record for juniors. Soon after, he joined the Indian Army as a junior commissioned officer, which gave him a salary and time to train.

Elbow surgery in 2019 kept him out for months. He worked with foreign coaches, including the German Uwe Hohn, the only man to throw the old javelin more than 100 metres, and the biomechanics expert Klaus Bartonietz.

{{story:sports-science}}

Scouting reached India in 1909, but at first only British and Anglo-Indian boys could join. Indians set up their own Scout groups in response. Leaders such as Annie Besant and Madan Mohan Malaviya helped start some of them.

After independence, the different groups joined together. The Bharat Scouts and Guides was formed in 1950 and is now one of the largest Scouting bodies in the world. Its members help at events such as the Kumbh Mela, where they guide lost pilgrims and give first aid.

{{story:north-star}}

2011 में मॉन्ट्रियल की मैक्गिल यूनिवर्सिटी के वैज्ञानिकों ने लोगों का दिमाग तब स्कैन किया जब वे अपने पसंदीदा संगीत के टुकड़े सुन रहे थे। हर व्यक्ति ने ऐसा संगीत चुना था जिससे उसे सिहरन होती थी। स्कैन में दिखा कि सबसे ऊँचे पलों पर डोपामिन छूट रहा था।

असली कमाल की खोज समय को लेकर थी। दिमाग के एक हिस्से ने चढ़ाव के दौरान, सबसे बढ़िया हिस्से से कुछ सेकंड पहले, डोपामिन छोड़ा। दूसरे हिस्से ने ठीक उस पल पर। दिमाग को अंदाज़ा लगाना अच्छा लगता है कि आगे क्या होगा, और सही निकलना भी, या फिर किसी अच्छे ढंग से चौंकना। फ़िल्मी [[@film-music-composers|संगीतकार]] इसी का इस्तेमाल करते हैं, जब कोई शांत दृश्य उठते-उठते एक ज़ोरदार सुर में फूट पड़ता है।

{{story:music-goosebumps}}

The idea of software "agents" that act for people is old. Computer scientists wrote about them in the 1990s, and simple ones sorted email or played games. But they could only follow fixed rules.

In November 2022, ChatGPT made talking AI famous around the world. Soon, companies began giving these models tools, so they could search the web, write and run code, and click buttons. By 2025 and 2026, AI agents could work for hours on a task, fix software bugs and even run tests on computer security.

{{story:ai-what}}

In the dry northwest of Rajasthan, near the town of [[@solar-india|Phalodi]], rows of [[@solar-panels|solar panels]] stretch across land where little else grows. This is Bhadla Solar Park, one of the largest solar farms on Earth. It covers about 56 square kilometres, roughly the size of 3,000 cricket grounds. Rajasthan gets strong sunshine on most days of the year, which makes it a good place to catch it.

The first practical silicon solar cell was shown at Bell Labs in the United States in 1954. It was costly and was used mostly on satellites. Since then, factories have learned to make panels so cheaply that the cost of solar electricity fell by roughly 90 per cent between 2010 and 2022. In many countries it is now among the cheapest kinds of new power you can build.

{{story:climate-change}}

Bhadla Solar Park, in the [[@thar-formed|Thar Desert]] of Rajasthan, is one of the largest solar parks anywhere. It covers about 56 square kilometres, an area bigger than many towns, with rows and rows of dark blue panels tilted towards the Sun. It can produce about 2,245 megawatts at full sunshine.

Deserts are good for solar parks because the land is flat and the skies are clear. But deserts are dusty, and dust blocks light. Many parks send small cleaning robots trundling along the panel rows at night. Workers also have to keep the panels from overheating, since very hot panels work a little less well.

{{story:photosynthesis}}

Kanishka is the most famous Kushan ruler. His main capital was Purushapura, today's Peshawar, and Mathura was another great centre. A stone [[inscription|inscription]] found at Rabatak in Afghanistan in 1993 lists his ancestors and names cities he controlled in northern India.

Buddhist tradition says Kanishka called a great Buddhist council in [[@kashmir|Kashmir]] and built an enormous [[stupa|stupa]] at Peshawar. Chinese pilgrims centuries later described that stupa as one of the tallest buildings they had seen. At Mathura, a life-size stone statue of Kanishka stands in the museum. Its head is missing, but his heavy boots and long coat show he dressed like a rider from the steppe, not like a local king.

{{story:indian-calendars}}

White left at the end of August 1587, expecting to return within months. But in 1588 Spain sent a huge war fleet, the Armada, against England. Every good ship was needed at home.

He returned on 18 August 1590, his granddaughter's third birthday. He found grass growing and his own chests dug up and ruined. Before leaving, he had agreed on a code. If the colonists moved, they would carve where they went. If they were in danger, they would add a cross. CROATOAN had no cross.

{{story:maps-history}}

The Viking longship was long, narrow and light, built of overlapping oak planks. With a square sail and rows of oars, it could cross open sea and then row up shallow rivers, or be dragged overland between them.

The Gokstad ship, found in a burial mound in Norway, is about 23 metres long, roughly the length of two school buses. In 1893, a copy of it crossed the Atlantic in about four weeks on its way to a world's fair in Chicago, showing how seaworthy the design was.

{{story:norse-myths}}

At immigration, an officer checks your [[@passports|passport]] and may scan it, take your photo or [[@fingerprints|fingerprints]], and stamp it. They may ask why you are visiting and how long you will stay.

At customs, bags may pass through [[@x-rays|X-ray machines]]. Trained dogs can sniff out drugs or hidden food. Many countries are strict about plants, fruit and meat, because they can carry pests and diseases. Australia, for example, can fine travellers for not declaring an apple, to protect its farms and wildlife.

{{story:passports}}

The festival takes its name from the great hornbill, a huge black-and-white bird with a casque, a hollow helmet-like bump, on top of its bill. [[@hornbills|Hornbills]] appear in many Naga folk songs and stories, and the bird is linked with courage and good fortune.

Traditionally, hornbill tail feathers decorated headdresses worn by warriors and dancers. Today, many groups use replica feathers instead, because the birds are protected. The great hornbill is the state bird of Kerala and Arunachal Pradesh, not Nagaland, but Nagaland made it the face of its biggest festival.

{{story:hornbills}}

The Mughal emperors loved Kashmir. They built terraced gardens on the slopes above Dal Lake, with water running down the middle in stone channels and fountains. Emperor Jahangir laid out Shalimar Bagh in 1619. Nishat Bagh, nearby, has twelve terraces climbing the hill.

These gardens followed a [[@ancient-persia|Persian idea]] of paradise as a walled garden full of flowing water. Huge chinar trees, a kind of plane tree, shade them. In autumn their leaves turn fiery red and orange, and the chinar leaf has become a symbol of Kashmir. Some chinars in the valley are said to be several hundred years old, planted in Mughal times.

{{story:saffron}}

In the Shinto religion, mountains are often seen as homes of spirits called kami. Fuji is linked with a goddess of blossoms, Konohanasakuya-hime, and many shrines are dedicated to her around the mountain. The summit [[crater|crater]] is treated as a holy place.

For centuries, groups of pilgrims climbed Fuji wearing white robes, ringing bells and chanting. Until 1872, women were officially banned from climbing to the top, though a few went up anyway. Today, climbers of every background go up, some still stopping at shrines on the way.

{{story:famous-paintings}}

Every item on a shelf carries hidden costs. A packet of [[@accident-potato-chips|chips]] includes the potatoes, oil, packaging, factory, advertising, truck ride and the shopkeeper's share. Each business along the way adds its own profit.

Shops usually work out a markup, a [[@percentages|percentage]] added on top of cost. Small, everyday things like bread or milk often have small markups, because shops sell lots of them. Fancy items, like a designer bag, may have much bigger ones.

{{story:percentages}}
